**Theorem 8.2 (normalization and all complex/reference/Borel identities).**
Definition 8.1 is a normalized actual finite CP acquisition with full
TP decoders and total encoder effect $I_5$. Forgetting all acquisition
labels gives exactly $\widehat\Phi_*$ on every complex five-mode
input, at every passive reference amplification and separately on every
original labelled Borel-time set.

Proof. The coefficient Gram gives, on all complex $A_V\in M_3$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_jc_jc_j^T&=L_0,\\
\sum_jK_jA_VK_j^\dagger
 &=\delta DA_VD^\dagger+TA_VT^\dagger+SA_VS^\dagger,\\
\sum_jK_j^\dagger K_j
 &=\delta D^\dagger D+T^\dagger T+S^\dagger S.
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.51}
$$

Range reconstruction gives precisely these products for the compressed
encoders followed by their isometric decoders, including their effects.
Adding the high pair makes the live coefficient Gram $I_3$ and live
effect $I_3-M$. The four scalar spectral effects sum to $M$, since
$\Pi_++\Pi_-=I_2$ and each report gets half. The two complement
effects sum to $I_5-P_V$. Thus

$$
\sum_{\text{actual }a}\mathcal E_a^\dagger(I)
=E(D^\dagger D+T^\dagger T+S^\dagger S+M)E^\dagger+(I_5-P_V)=I_5.
\tag{EP.52}
$$

Every finite encoder is CP/TNI because its positive effect is bounded
by this sum. The live decoders are isometries on the ENTIRE declared
fibres. Scalar decoders are TP since
$\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal L}w_\ell=1$ and
$\int_0^1 2/(1+t)^2dt=[-2/(1+t)]_0^1=1$. No branch normalization or
survival division is involved.

For every complex $A\in M_5$, let $A_V=E^\dagger AE$. The forgotten
maps, not just their effects or test-state values, are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi^L(A)&=E(DA_VD^\dagger+TA_VT^\dagger+SA_VS^\dagger)E^\dagger,\\
\Phi^f_\ell(A)(B)&=G_B\left[
 w_\ell\operatorname{tr}(EME^\dagger A)
 +\mathbf1_{\ell=2}\operatorname{tr}((I_5-P_V)A)\right]P_f,\\
G_B&=\int_B\frac{2\,dt}{(1+t)^2},\qquad
B\subseteq[0,1]\text{ Borel},\quad\ell\in\mathcal L=(0,1,2,3,13).
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.53}
$$

The live equation follows from the total coefficient Gram and image
reconstruction; the failure equation from spectral effect $M$ and the
complement effect. They hold on every complex matrix unit. Cross-support
operators between $P_V$ and $I_5-P_V$ have zero output. Wrong support
and additional output sectors vanish. Both zero-weight labels stay zero;
labels $1,2,3$ remain separate. No singular or endpoint-atomic measure
is added. All common metadata retain their fixed state.

For a joint complex operator $X$ with an arbitrary passive reference,
tensor every finite live Kraus map and decoder with $I_R$. The live
output is

$$
\sum_{K\in\{D,T,S\}}(EKE^\dagger\otimes I_R)X
                                  (EK^\dagger E^\dagger\otimes I_R).
\tag{EP.54}
$$

The reference operators in the two failure terms are exactly

$$
\Theta_M(X)=\operatorname{Tr}_5[(EME^\dagger\otimes I_R)X],\qquad
\Theta_\perp(X)=\operatorname{Tr}_5[((I_5-P_V)\otimes I_R)X].
\tag{EP.55}
$$

Thus the amplified failure output is
$G_BP_f\otimes[w_\ell\Theta_M(X)+\mathbf1_{\ell=2}\Theta_\perp(X)]$.
Each spectral row first supplies its own conditional reference operator;
their sum is $\Theta_M$. Each complement label supplies
$\Theta_\perp/2$. These are not unconditional reference marginals.
This proves the full complex/reference/separately labelled Borel identity,
without time binning. The density $g$ is the actual approximate density;
it does not replace any ideal $f_y$ in EP.2. Equality to the same
complete approximate channel reuses the conditional original HALF
full-cq error certificate for all twelve histories, including height
$16$ and every live coherence. Necessity never prescribed this channel
to competitors. $\square$

